Q7 – Diagnose, Redesign and Validate 
Selected solution: Q4 – Pharmacy Inventory Management
I am selecting the HashMap-based pharmacy inventory system from Q4.

The original implementation uses a HashMap for storing medicine records. When a report requires medicine codes in alphabetical order, it collects the keys and sorts them every time the report is generated.

This creates unnecessary repeated work when the hospital generates the same type of report multiple times without changing its inventory.

My redesign will retain the HashMap for fast medicine lookup but introduce a cached sorted report. The sorted list will be generated only when required and reused until the inventory changes.

A. Diagnose the Bottleneck 
In the original Q4 implementation, HashMap provides efficient insertion and lookup. However, it does not maintain medicine codes in sorted order.

Whenever a code-ordered report is requested, the program must:
1. Retrieve all medicine codes.
2. Copy them into a list.
3. Sort the entire list.
4. Display the sorted result.
The main bottleneck is the repeated sorting operation.

Time complexity:
Operation	Original implementation
Insert medicine	O(1) average
Search medicine	O(1) average
Generate sorted report	O(n log n)
Generate 100 sorted reports	O(100 × n log n)

Here, n is the number of medicines.

For example, if the inventory contains 100,000 medicines and the application generates 100 reports without any inventory changes, the same medicine codes are sorted 100 times.

Diagnosis: The limitation comes from the unordered nature of HashMap. It does not preserve sorted order, so the program repeats sorting work unnecessarily.
B. Define the Design Objective – 2 Marks
The redesign should achieve the following objectives:
1. Maintain fast medicine insertion and lookup.
2. Avoid sorting the same medicine codes repeatedly.
3. Generate the sorted report only when the inventory changes.
4. Preserve the correctness of alphabetical ordering.
5. Reduce report-generation time when multiple reports are requested.
Measurable objective:

For an inventory of 100,000 medicines and 100 consecutive report requests without inventory updates:
- Original design: perform sorting 100 times.
- Redesigned solution: perform sorting only once.
The goal is to reduce repeated sorting while maintaining O(1) average-time medicine lookup.
C. Design the Revised Solution – 4 Marks
Proposed redesign: HashMap with cached sorted keys
Instead of replacing the original HashMap, I will improve its design by adding a cached sorted list.

The HashMap continues to store the actual medicine records. The cached list stores medicine codes in alphabetical order.
Revised system design
Pharmacy Inventory System


HashMap

Stores medicine records and supports fast lookup.


Cached sorted list

Stores medicine codes in alphabetical order.




Generate sorted report

Reuse the cached list until the inventory changes.
How the redesigned solution works
1. When a medicine is inserted, it is stored in the HashMap.
2. The cached sorted list is marked invalid if a new medicine code is inserted.
3. When a sorted report is requested, the program checks whether the cache is valid.
4. If the cache is invalid, the program sorts the medicine codes and stores the result.
5. If the cache is already valid, the program directly reuses the sorted list.
Pseudocode
CREATE HashMap
CREATE cachedSortedList
SET cacheValid = false

FUNCTION addMedicine(code, medicine):
    IF code is new:
        insert medicine into HashMap
        cacheValid = false
    ELSE:
        update existing medicine

FUNCTION searchMedicine(code):
    RETURN HashMap.get(code)

FUNCTION generateSortedReport():
    IF cacheValid is false:
        cachedSortedList = sort(HashMap keys)
        cacheValid = true

    RETURN cachedSortedList


What changes in the redesign?
Feature	Original	Redesigned
Medicine storage	HashMap	HashMap
Medicine lookup	HashMap lookup	HashMap lookup
Sorted report	Sort every time	Sort only when needed
Repeated reports	Repeated sorting	Reuse cached result
Extra memory	Temporary sorting list	Cached sorted list

Trade-offs:
- The redesigned system requires additional O(n) memory for the cached list.
- When a new medicine is inserted, the cache must be invalidated.
- The first report after an inventory update still requires O(n log n) sorting.
- Subsequent reports avoid sorting.
This approach is useful when reports are requested frequently but inventory updates are less frequent.
D. Predict Before Implementing – 1 Mark
Before implementation, I predict that the redesigned solution will perform better when multiple sorted reports are generated without changing the inventory.
Measurement	Original	Redesigned
First sorted report	O(n log n)	O(n log n)
Subsequent sorted report	O(n log n)	O(n) to traverse/display cached keys
Sorting operations for 100 reports	100	1
Additional memory	Temporary list	Cached list of O(n)

Prediction: The redesigned solution should significantly reduce total report-generation time when the same inventory is reported repeatedly.

E. Implement and Validate the Redesign – 2 Marks
The following Java program compares both implementations using identical inventory sizes and report counts.

Save it as InventoryRedesign.java.

import java.util.*;

public class InventoryRedesign {

    // Original implementation:
    // Sorts medicine codes every time a report is requested.
    static class OriginalInventory {
        HashMap<String, String> medicines = new HashMap<>();
        int sortCount = 0;

        void addMedicine(String code, String name) {
            medicines.put(code, name);
        }

        List<String> sortedReport() {
            List<String> codes =
                new ArrayList<>(medicines.keySet());

            Collections.sort(codes);
            sortCount++;

            return codes;
        }
    }

    // Redesigned implementation:
    // Reuses the sorted list until inventory changes.
    static class ImprovedInventory {
        HashMap<String, String> medicines = new HashMap<>();
        List<String> cachedCodes = null;
        boolean cacheValid = false;
        int sortCount = 0;

        void addMedicine(String code, String name) {
            if (!medicines.containsKey(code)) {
                medicines.put(code, name);
                cacheValid = false;
            } else {
                medicines.put(code, name);
            }
        }

        List<String> sortedReport() {
            if (!cacheValid) {
                cachedCodes =
                    new ArrayList<>(medicines.keySet());

                Collections.sort(cachedCodes);
                sortCount++;
                cacheValid = true;
            }

            return cachedCodes;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] sizes = {1000, 10000, 100000};
        int reportCount = 100;

        System.out.println(
            "Size\tOriginal(ms)\tRedesigned(ms)\tSorts Original\tSorts Redesigned"
        );

        for (int n : sizes) {

            OriginalInventory original =
                new OriginalInventory();

            ImprovedInventory improved =
                new ImprovedInventory();

            // Create identical inventories.
            for (int i = n; i >= 1; i--) {
                String code = String.format("MED%06d", i);

                original.addMedicine(code, "Medicine" + i);
                improved.addMedicine(code, "Medicine" + i);
            }

            // Warm-up both implementations.
            original.sortedReport();
            improved.sortedReport();

            original.sortCount = 0;
            improved.sortCount = 0;

            long startOriginal = System.nanoTime();

            List<String> originalResult = null;

            for (int i = 0; i < reportCount; i++) {
                originalResult = original.sortedReport();
            }

            long endOriginal = System.nanoTime();

            long startImproved = System.nanoTime();

            List<String> improvedResult = null;

            for (int i = 0; i < reportCount; i++) {
                improvedResult = improved.sortedReport();
            }

            long endImproved = System.nanoTime();

            // Verify that both produce identical reports.
            if (!originalResult.equals(improvedResult)) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Report mismatch!");
            }

            double originalTime =
                (endOriginal - startOriginal) / 1_000_000.0;

            double improvedTime =
                (endImproved - startImproved) / 1_000_000.0;

            System.out.printf(
                "%d\t%.3f\t\t%.3f\t\t%d\t\t%d%n",
                n,
                originalTime,
                improvedTime,
                original.sortCount,
                improved.sortCount
            );
        }

        System.out.println(
            "\nBoth implementations generated identical reports."
        );
    }
}



Experimental conditions
- Both implementations use the same medicine codes.
- Both use the same inventory sizes.
- Both generate 100 reports.
- Both use the same sorting method.
- The inventory is not modified during the 100 report requests.
- The program verifies that both implementations generate identical sorted reports.
