F. Defend the Final Design – 2 Marks
The redesigned solution addresses the original bottleneck by avoiding repeated sorting.
Evaluation
Evaluation factor	Result
Correctness	Both solutions produce the same sorted report.
Repeated sorting	Reduced from 100 sorts to 1.
Lookup performance	Retains HashMap's O(1) average lookup.
Memory usage	Increases by O(n) for the cached list.
First report after update	Still requires O(n log n) sorting.
Subsequent reports	Avoid sorting and reuse the cache.

When is the original solution preferable?
The original implementation may still be preferable when:
- Reports are generated very rarely.
- Inventory changes frequently.
- Memory is extremely limited.
- The application does not benefit from caching sorted results.
The redesigned solution is more suitable when the pharmacy frequently generates reports and the inventory remains relatively stable between reports.
Final conclusion
The redesign improves the original pharmacy inventory system without sacrificing its fast HashMap lookup performance. Instead of sorting medicine codes every time a report is requested, it sorts them once and reuses the result until the inventory changes.

The expected improvement is a reduction from 100 sorting operations to just one for 100 unchanged report requests. It uses additional O(n) memory, but this trade-off is reasonable when reports are generated frequently.

Therefore, the redesigned HashMap with cached sorted keys is the better engineering choice for a pharmacy system with frequent reporting and relatively stable inventory data.
